---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { PAGES, GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL, EMAIL } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={PAGES.about.title} description={PAGES.about.description} image="/og/about.png" imageAlt="About Pavel Luzanov" />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="about-page">
				<h1>About Me</h1>
				<div class="about-content">
					<p>
						I started as a frontend engineer, and for years that was my title. The job
						was never only the browser, though. From my first role I was in the whole
						cycle: arguing about API schemas, talking to product managers, designers and
						other engineers, and coordinating the work as far as anyone would let me.
						Frontend is still where my craft is strongest, with years of build systems,
						bundle sizes and design kits behind it, and it shapes how I look at any
						product.
					</p>
					<p>
						Then the ground moved, and the part I had always done on the side became the main job. Once agents started writing most of the code, the
						scarce thing stopped being a pair of hands in React or in any other
						language. It became the person who understands the domain, sits across from
						the business and works out what they really want, turns that into a
						specification an agent can follow, and keeps the whole machine pointed in
						one direction. That is the work I found myself doing, and the work I want
						to keep doing.
					</p>
					<p>
						Lately it has taken me far from the browser. Most of the last year went
						into the backend and into speed, making CI and delivery faster. I never
						learned to write backend code by hand, and I will not pretend otherwise. But
						I live inside it, I know why it is built the way it is, and I answer for
						what ships.
					</p>
					<p>
						What ties it together is a stubborn habit: I take a product to release,
						whatever that takes. Sometimes it is a design kit, sometimes a spec,
						sometimes a pipeline that is far too slow. I write about how it works in
						practice on the blog, starting with a small protocol that helps agents find
						their way around a repository.
					</p>
					<h2>Writing</h2>
					<p>
						Engineering articles from 2021 and 2022 and recent notes on agent workflows live
						on the <a href="/blog">blog</a>.
					</p>
					<p>
						Feel free to reach out if you'd like to collaborate or talk about engineering.
					</p>
					<div class="contact-links">
						<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`} class="btn btn-primary">Email Me</a>
						<a href={GITHUB_URL} class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
						<a href={LINKEDIN_URL} class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.about-page {
		max-width: 700px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 2rem 1rem;
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: 2.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.about-content {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		line-height: 1.8;
	}

	.about-content p {
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}

	h2 {
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
	}

	.contact-links {
		display: flex;
		gap: 1rem;
		justify-content: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin-top: 2rem;
	}

</style>
